--TEST--
An un-narrowed `?array<T>` is refused at the `foreach` subject rather than iterated when it happens to hold an array, and narrowing it is what makes the same loop compile — `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects`, `rule:php-migration/absent-storage-is-never-a-zero-value`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects`'s list names an `array<T>`, and
// `null|array<T>` is not one: the nullable half has no elements to yield, so
// the subject is refused where it is written rather than skipped at run time
// the way PHP's `foreach (null as $v)` warns and continues. That refusal is
// what `nvs-ir`'s array `foreach` lowering is guarded by — a nullable subject
// that reached it would have no `array<T>` to walk.
//
// Both spellings are here because the sugar and the union have to agree: a
// shape refused as `?array<string>` and admitted as `null|array<string>`
// would make the fix depend on which one the author wrote.
class Bag {
    public ?array<string> $items = null;
}

?array<string> $maybe = ["a", "b"];
array<string>|null $spelled = ["c"];
Bag $bag = new Bag();

foreach ($maybe as string $v) { echo $v; }
foreach ($spelled as string $w) { echo $w; }
foreach ($bag->items as string $x) { echo $x; }

// The narrowed loop is the fix the refusal leaves open, and it is deliberately
// last: a session that made the three above compile would have to delete this
// line's reason for existing too.
if ($maybe != null) {
    foreach ($maybe as string $y) { echo $y; }
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0443]: `foreach` cannot iterate a `null|array<string>`
%A
error[E0443]: `foreach` cannot iterate a `null|array<string>`
%A
error[E0443]: `foreach` cannot iterate a `null|array<string>`
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
